ent for  microbes so looking at humans and  biomedical models agricultural models  you can see soybean here  these are the types of projects we do  but what unifies them is this interest  in the microorganisms how those  microorganisms interact with these  either environments or or eukaryotic  organisms  and then ultimately how the viruses that  infect those microbes uh play into that  so it's kind of a tripartite system  we're looking at  and to to study this you know obviously  dna you know those types of omics  techniques are the bread and butter of  looking at these types of systems uh so  those are really kind of the unifying  themes that go through my research  so  you know just as a basic background  viruses are really difficult to study if  you can go out and isolate a virus and  sequence this genome you know you're  you're golden you can do a lot of  research with it but it is very very  hard to do that even when we know what  the host is say human it is often very  difficult to go out and find specific  viruses that are infecting that host  much less if you're looking at  environmental samples where there could  be thousands of potential hosts and  thousands or tens of thousands of  different types of viruses and figuring  out which virus goes with which host and  what can you host can you culture the  host can you culture the virus  so there's a there's a lot of  complexity in trying to study viruses  so  dna based methods have really become  important for looking at viruses  however a lot of the tools that allow us  to use omics based techniques and other  organisms like marker genes that are  universal say the 16s rna gene that gets  used in bacteria don't exist in viruses  there is no one gene that every virus  has  so it makes it quite difficult quite  quite complex to look at  so using shotgun metagenomics which you  know would be going out into an  environment taking a sample that has a  broad mixture of organisms in it  typically what we'll do is we will  filter out the cellular organisms into a  separate fraction so that what's left is  just things of a size that should be  viruses  and then we sequence that entire sample  right so we get a huge amount of dna  sequencing data back and it's about many  many many different types of viruses  that might be living in an individual  sample  so then we use various techniques to go  through and look at that and some of  those techniques can be quite  computationally expensive  but you know as computing and other  technologies have advanced that's really  not a limiting factor in doing these  types of  uh of techniques at this point at this  point really our limiting factor is our  ability to make biological  interpretations out of those genomic  data that we get  i'd say in viruses it's really hard  because as i mentioned you can't culture  many of them so for us to actually have  phenotypes in the viruses that we could  potentially correlate with things we're  seeing genotypically in that metagenomic  data it becomes a real challenge for us  so  you know  other other issues that we run into are  things like if we look at any given  environment and we metagenomically  sequence it and then we cluster all the  putative proteins that are there  and we look at them  from the most common to the least common  among the most common proteins one study  we have here we found that 62 of the 100  most abundant putative proteins were  uncharacterized  and of the ones which is saying they  were not in a genome at that point  and of the ones that were actually in a  genome all the ones that have arrows  pointing to them here were you know they  were annotated as puted of  uncharacterized protein which might as  well be  uncharacterized uh or are unknown uh for  for all intensive purposes so this is a  major wall we also run into is that you  know there's a lot of genomic data out  there but we don't even know what gene  it corresponds to so much less what type  of activity it may