tand some of  these genes that we see very commonly in  uh  the the various metagenome sequences and  you know i  we've definitely looked at both unknown  and known functional genes through these  types of methods and we have interesting  stories on both  today i'm going to follow through with  one story that is from a known gene  which would be dna polymerase one so if  you're not familiar it is the  proofreading polymerase and many  bacteria but in  viruses that carry out it it is the main  polymerase it is the one they use for  replicating their genome  and it is a fairly common gene for a  double-stranded dna virus to carry we  see it in about 25  of known dna viruses double-stranded dna  viruses so that's actually a pretty good  percentage for us so looking at this  gene gives us the ability to look across  a lot of different viruses  so one group that has looked at this and  there are a couple of grad students just  chopin and dan nasco  also eric sakowski who's not on here and  in particular one undergrad helen  schmidt  who really adopted this dna polymerase  gene over the course of several years  and made some really interesting  discoveries with it that i wanted to  walk you through  so  first is a little bit of background to  remind you  viruses and microbial viruses in  particular tend to come in two  lifestyles or flavors  there are the viruses that will enter a  cell and immediately they're going to go  into the replicative cycle make a lot of  copies of themselves and immediately  lyse the cell so those are the ones that  are virulent or live the light the lytic  lifestyle by default  the other group are the temperate phage  or temperate viruses those are the ones  that enter the cell incorporate  themselves into the host genome  will lie dormant for some period of time  and then eventually some trigger in the  cell or the cell's environment will  cause it to essentially activate itself  and then do a replication so  understanding which viruses are lytic  and lysogenic  is a pretty pretty important thing when  you're trying to understand viral  biology and how viruses impact their  host  so  it seems like this would be a very easy  thing to identify within a sequence but  it's not always and actually many many  many phage that are out there we have  complete genome sequences but we still  don't know for sure which one of these  lifestyles uh those phage tend to follow  so  uh helen schmidt as i mentioned she was  a undergraduate biochemistry major uh  and she came into one of our summer uh  undergraduate internship uh  opportunities and adopted this dna  polymerase one uh cluster and started  looking at it in much more detail  um and through her work and her  particular interest in biochemistry uh  she noticed something within this gene  that has led to 12 years of subsequent  research for us  and that was the fact that within this  gene  when you look at the the protein  sequence for it there is an individual  amino acid residue within it that seems  to be predictive for the phage lifestyle  if we look at this  residue at position 762 relative to the  e coli version of the gene  the the default that you tend to see in  bacteria as a phenylalanine at that  position  however if tyrosine is at that position  uh there is biochemical data out there  that indicates that that makes the  polymerase about a thousand times more  processive faster  essentially but also makes it more prone  to errors while it's replicating  um there's also a mutation that's been  biochemically characterized to leucine  at that location and when that amino  acid is in there we get about three  times more accurate but about a thousand  times slower than the phenylalanine  so it's quite a spread in the speed and  the accuracy that occurs  based on this one amino acid change in  polymerases and what helen noticed was  that this individual amino acid residue  when she overlaid its identity onto a  tree  very neatly clades with our groups with  