tand some of  these genes that we see very commonly in  uh  the the various metagenome sequences and  you know i  we've definitely looked at both unknown  and known functional genes through these  types of methods and we have interesting  stories on both  today i'm going to follow through with  one story that is from a known gene  which would be dna polymerase one so if  you're not familiar it is the  proofreading polymerase and many  bacteria but in  viruses that carry out it it is the main  polymerase it is the one they use for  replicating their genome  and it is a fairly common gene for a  double-stranded dna virus to carry we  see it in about 25  of known dna viruses double-stranded dna  viruses so that's actually a pretty good  percentage for us so looking at this  gene gives us the ability to look across  a lot of different viruses  so one group that has looked at this and  there are a couple of grad students just  chopin and dan nasco  also eric sakowski who's not on here and  in particular one undergrad helen  schmidt  who really adopted this dna polymerase  gene over the course of several years  and made some really interesting  discoveries with it that i wanted to  walk you through  so  first is a little bit of background to  remind you  viruses and microbial viruses in  particular tend to come in two  lifestyles or flavors  there are the viruses that will enter a  cell and immediately they're going to go  into the replicative cycle make a lot of  copies of themselves and immediately  lyse the cell so those are the ones that  are virulent or live the light the lytic  lifestyle by default  the other group are the temperate phage  or temperate viruses those are the ones  that enter the cell incorporate  themselves into the host genome  will lie dormant for some period of time  and then eventually some trigger in the  cell or the cell's environment will  cause it to essentially activate itself  and then do a replication so  understanding which viruses are lytic  and lysogenic  is a pretty pretty important thing when  you're trying to understand viral  biology and how viruses impact their  host  so  it seems like this would be a very easy  thing to identify within a sequence but  it's not always and actually many many  many phage that are out there we have  complete genome sequences but we still  don't know for sure which one of these  lifestyles uh those phage tend to follow  so  uh helen schmidt as i mentioned she was  a undergraduate biochemistry major uh  and she came into one of our summer uh  undergraduate internship uh  opportunities and adopted this dna  polymerase one uh cluster and started  looking at it in much more detail  um and through her work and her  particular interest in biochemistry uh  she noticed something within this gene  that has led to 12 years of subsequent  research for us  and that was the fact that within this  gene  when you look at the the protein  sequence for it there is an individual  amino acid residue within it that seems  to be predictive for the phage lifestyle  if we look at this  residue at position 762 relative to the  e coli version of the gene  the the default that you tend to see in  bacteria as a phenylalanine at that  position  however if tyrosine is at that position  uh there is biochemical data out there  that indicates that that makes the  polymerase about a thousand times more  processive faster  essentially but also makes it more prone  to errors while it's replicating  um there's also a mutation that's been  biochemically characterized to leucine  at that location and when that amino  acid is in there we get about three  times more accurate but about a thousand  times slower than the phenylalanine  so it's quite a spread in the speed and  the accuracy that occurs  based on this one amino acid change in  polymerases and what helen noticed was  that this individual amino acid residue  when she overlaid its identity onto a  tree  very neatly clades with our groups with  the clades that were on the tree we can  cleanly see there are two groups here  that are leucine but there's a single  group each for phenylalanine and  tyrosine so to us that was an indication  wow there is a real  uh biological uh  phenomenon going on with this amino acid  residue  and we really saw that when she started  to look at the lifestyle of the phage  that we did know their um their  lifestyle and that was that the the  tyrosine version was always lytic we we  had no exceptions at the time of a  lysogenic uh phage that had tyrosine  phenylalanine was almost always lytic a  few exceptions there  and leucine was almost always lysogenic  so just this individual residue gave us  that information that otherwise is very  very difficult for us to identify out of  these types of sequences  so  we just do the thought of experiment of  why this might be important to viral  biology  let's think about you know lydic viruses  are the ones that come into their host  they replicate fast they produce lots of  copies of themselves and then they're  out of the cell  so for those guys if they have errors in  a few copies maybe that's okay maybe  occasionally you're going to get a  mutant and maybe that mutant is actually  a good thing right maybe that's new  diversity that's going to allow them to  succeed but if that mutant actually is  bad then you know it it's okay you've  got lots of copies that are faithful to  the original  um  you know and occasionally you're going  to get a really crazy mutation which uh  you know may may or may not be a good  thing but you have to see how that works  out with natural selection but the  really interesting thing with this trait  is you're modifying your polymerase and  your polymerase is ultimately  responsible for the mutations that could  lead to natural selection lead to  evolution so really by modifying their  polymerase in this way or you know  naturally selecting for the polymerase  in this way they're evolving to increase  the rate of of evolution which you know  that's a phenomenal thought right  they're they're they're really giving  themselves the ability to be more  diverse  if we look at the other side of the of  the coin temperate phage tend to  replicate slower they're sitting within  their cell for a long time so they  really need to be more in sync with the  cell and then when they actually do  start to replicate themselves they're  doing it at a critical time when there's  probably a stress on the cell and they  really need to get accurate copies and  get out so that they can survive and  reinfect a new cell so  in those phage you know selecting for a  more accurate mutation this leucine  mutation even though it's a bit slower  it may have significant evolutionary uh  of  potential for you know keeping it in  sync with its host  so for the rest of my talk or actually  one more slide on this so you know the  the the first students i talked about  they were doing that process that whole  process very manually we've actually  since done a lot of work on streamlining  it making it easier building  bioinformatics tools i've had a number  of grad students who have come through  and we've just made an entire suite of  bioinformatics tools that make this  process of investigating these viral  genes in this way  very straightforward  and easy to do um  so  for the rest of the talk if we go back  to this dna polymerase a you know we  we've looked at plenty of other clusters  but this one has really been one that  has really captured our  our interest  uh and i i mentioned this this thousand  times faster but not quite as accurate  or three times more accurate and a  thousand times slower  um those are really you know fairly  widespread in biochemical literature but  when we really traced it back they all  went back to single studies where they  tried some in vitro uh  mutations so really we didn't know for  sure that all polymerases followed this  rule or that it's something we would  ac